{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgfbr4NxYYSR5kRtrjyNp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Approach.** For d⁷ in Td the ground state is ⁴A₂(F). The three spin-allowed bands are:\n- ν₁ = ⁴T₂(F) ← ⁴A₂, which is 10Dq.\n- ν₂ = ⁴T₁(F) ← ⁴A₂.\n- ν₃ = ⁴T₁(P) ← ⁴A₂.\n\nThe two ⁴T₁ states mix, so ν₂ and ν₃ depend on both Dq and B. Measured from the ⁴F baseline, the ⁴T₁ matrix is [[6Dq, 4B], [4B, 15B]]. The ground state sits at −12Dq. I took the off-diagonal term as 4B from memory of the Tanabe–Sugano matrix, and I did not verify it. The result depends on that value.\n\nRelative to the ground state, the two ⁴T₁ energies are:\n\nν₂,₃ = 15Dq + 7.5B ∓ √[(7.5B − 3Dq)² + 16B²]\n\n**Solution.**\n1. Adding the two roots gives ν₂ + ν₃ = 30Dq + 15B = 23 430. So Dq = 781 − B/2.\n2. The splitting gives ν₃ − ν₂ = 9 370. So (9B − 2343)² + 16B² = 4685².\n3. This simplifies to 97B² − 42 174B − 1.646×10⁷ = 0. Its positive root is B ≈ 683 cm⁻¹.\n4. Then Dq ≈ 781 − 342 ≈ 439 cm⁻¹.\n\n**Check.**\n- The fit predicts ν₁ = 10Dq ≈ 4 390 cm⁻¹. The observed band is at 4 070 cm⁻¹, so it is about 8% off.\n- The fit also reproduces ν₂ ≈ 7 030 cm⁻¹ and ν₃ ≈ 16 400 cm⁻¹ by construction.\n- B ≈ 683 cm⁻¹ is about 70% of the free-ion value of roughly 970 cm⁻¹. That is reasonable for a tetrahedral Co(II) complex.\n\n**Result:** Dq ≈ 440 cm⁻¹ (10Dq ≈ 4 400 cm⁻¹) and B ≈ 680 cm⁻¹.\n\nIf ν₁ alone were used, Dq would be 407 cm⁻¹. That value is not consistent with ν₂ and ν₃ under the 4B coupling, so I used the ν₂/ν₃ fit.\n\n{\"Dq\": 439, \"B\": 683, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Dq and B fitted from ν2 and ν3 with ⁴T1(F)–⁴T1(P) mixing (off-diagonal 4B, from memory); predicted ν1 is about 4390 cm⁻¹ against 4070 observed, and C cannot be determined from spin-allowed bands.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1648,"cach